{
  "example": "In `src/lib.rs`, `fn outer() { fn inner() {} }` gives the selectors `fn[outer]` and `fn[outer]/fn[inner]`. {cite}`requirements.references.rust_item_selectors`",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "The example is covered by tests that use its exact scenario.\n\n**Scenario:** In `src/lib.rs`, `fn outer() { fn inner() {} }` gives the selectors `fn[outer]` and `fn[outer]/fn[inner]`.\n\n**Tests marked `{test}` for `selector_nested`:**\n- `rust_selectors_follow_syntactic_ancestry` in `src/outline/tests.rs` (lines 35–100) parses the line `fn outer() { fn inner() {} }` as `src/lib.rs`.\n  - It asserts that the collected selectors contain `fn[outer]/fn[inner]`.\n  - It then calls `outline.resolve(\"fn[outer]/fn[inner]\")` and asserts that the resolved node's `selector` equals that string.\n- `selectors_name_syntactic_ancestry_and_duplicates` (lines 520–564) uses the same source line and asserts that `fn[outer]/fn[inner]` is among the selectors.\n\n**The `fn[outer]` half:**\n- No marked test lists `fn[outer]` alone, but the check is still made.\n- `Outline::resolve` in `src/outline/mod.rs` walks from the root. At each step it finds a child whose segment equals the wanted segment. So `fn[outer]/fn[inner]` can only resolve if the root has a child with segment `fn[outer]` and that child has a child with segment `fn[inner]`.\n- The nested node's selector is built from its parent's selector, so the first test confirms both selectors.\n- The unmarked test `line_queries_find_innermost_and_declared_nodes` also asserts the node selector `fn[outer]` directly.\n\n**Documentation:** `md/reference.md` documents the selector spelling and lists `fn[outer]/fn[inner]` among its examples.\n\nThe tests exercise the exact scenario and its expected outcomes, so I judge the example covered.",
  "inputs": [
    {
      "requirements": "requirements.references.selector_nested",
      "matches": [
        "requirements.references.selector_nested"
      ],
      "text": [
        ":::{example} selector_nested",
        "In `src/lib.rs`, `fn outer() { fn inner() {} }` gives the selectors `fn[outer]` and `fn[outer]/fn[inner]`. {cite}`requirements.references.rust_item_selectors`",
        ":::"
      ]
    },
    {
      "requirements": "requirements.references.rust_item_selectors",
      "matches": [
        "requirements.references.rust_item_selectors"
      ],
      "text": [
        ":::{req} rust_item_selectors",
        "The Rust outline profile under {cite}`requirements.references.outlines` MUST",
        "identify items by segments describing their syntactic ancestry within the file,",
        "such as `fn[run]`, `struct[Packet]`, and `mod[local]/fn[run]`, and MUST NOT",
        "resolve Rust modules across files. Functions, methods, structs, enums, unions,",
        "traits, impls, modules, macros, constants, statics, type aliases, and extern",
        "blocks MUST be outline nodes. Impl segments MUST be named by their syntactic self",
        "type and optional trait path, as `impl[Thing]` and `impl[Thing/Trait]`. `use`",
        "declarations and other items without a suitable name MUST be anonymous. Selector",
        "spelling MUST be documented.",
        ":::"
      ]
    },
